# adoption-ledger.surfaces — WHICH SURFACES A NAMED REFERENCE ALREADY ANSWERED.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
# ===========================================================================
# One record per AREA of the adoption ledger. An area is a surface of our own
# code that somebody has already read a proven third-party project against and
# written down a verdict for. A dispatch that BUILDS on such a surface must
# cite the row, so the engineer starts from the verdict instead of re-deriving
# it — CEO ruling, 2026-09-19, in his words:
#
#     "In other words, you ARE FUCKING REINVENTING THE FUCKING WHEEL FROM
#      SCRATCH"
#
# guard-reference-ledger.sh is the only consumer. It reads this file and
# nothing else: the signals below are DATA, so adding an area is an edit here
# and never an edit to a guard.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE RECORD FORMAT
# ===========================================================================
# Blank-line separated records, `key: value` per line, `#` comments. The same
# shape owned-systems.declaration and spawn-guard-audience.declaration use,
# deliberately: two declaration formats in one engine is one format too many.
#
#   ledger:       (header record, exactly one) the document that carries the
#                 verdicts, as a repository-relative path.
#   ledger-repo:  the repository it lives in. The guard opens the document to
#                 check that a cited section EXISTS; where it cannot be found,
#                 a citation is accepted on its shape and logged UNVERIFIED.
#                 "I cannot check" is never "forbidden".
#   ledger-name:  what a citation may call it, besides the file name.
#
#   area:     the row's id. Appears in the refusal.
#   section:  the ledger section to cite, e.g. 2.5.
#   verdict:  the ledger's own verdict, quoted back in the refusal so the
#             engineer learns the answer from the refusal itself.
#   paths:    comma-separated PATH FRAGMENTS of OUR code. STRONG — one match
#             inside a build section fires the row. A fragment is matched
#             literally and must be specific enough to name one surface:
#             `run-tests.sh` is a path, `tests` is not.
#   weak-paths:
#             the same, for a file that the row's surface SHARES with other
#             work. A weak path fires only when one of the row's `terms` also
#             appears somewhere in the prompt. MEASURED, not invented:
#             `ui/phone.js` began life as a strong path and fired on 13 phone
#             briefs of 2026-09-19, twelve of which were about button focus,
#             scroll position and splash placement. Exactly ONE of the
#             thirteen carried a transport word anywhere in its text, and it
#             is the one the CEO's ruling is about.
#   terms:    comma-separated PHRASES. STRONG, and therefore every one of them
#             must be a phrase somebody only writes when working on this
#             surface. Single ordinary words — port, scratch, lint, sign,
#             retry, publish — are DELIBERATELY ABSENT: the brief that ordered
#             this guard proposed exactly those, they were measured against 288
#             real briefs and 37 real spawn prompts, and every one of them fired
#             on text about something else. The measurement is in
#             guard-reference-ledger.test.sh's header.
#
# A row may share a path with another row; the refusal names every row that
# matched, because an engineer touching run-tests.sh is standing on the harness
# verdict AND the developer-loop verdict at once.

ledger: docs/research/t3code-tooling-what-to-adopt-2026-09-19.md
ledger-repo: richos-hq
ledger-name: adoption ledger

area: build-sign-notarize-publish
section: 2.1
verdict: COPY THE APPROACH (three named designs); NOT APPLICABLE (the code)
paths: app/scripts/nightly-local.py, app/scripts/make-release.sh, app/scripts/package-app, app/scripts/verify-engine-asset-members.sh
terms: notarize, notarization, code signing, hardened runtime, bundle invariant, staple the notarization

area: update-channel-and-rollback
section: 2.2
verdict: COPY THE APPROACH, narrowly — our updater is NOT behind theirs on transactionality
paths: crates/richos-user-update, richos-user-update/src
terms: update channel, updater manifest, prepared update, trial migration, auto-update, differential update, delta download

area: test-harness-and-screenshot-stability
section: 2.3
verdict: NOT APPLICABLE on screenshots (we are ahead); ADOPT AS-IS on the isolation rule
paths: app/scripts/run-tests.sh, ui/tests/lib/harness.js, ui/tests/lib/shot-stability.js
terms: test retries, retry the test, flaky test, flakiness, screenshot stability, visual regression, file parallelism, shard the suite, reference screenshot

area: release-gating
section: 2.4
verdict: COPY THE APPROACH — the highest value-per-day item in the ledger
paths: .github/workflows/ci.yml, .github/workflows/release.yml
terms: release gating, release smoke, release-only step, nightly channel, promote the nightly, preview channel, timeout-minutes, continue-on-error

area: developer-loop-shared-resources
section: 2.5
verdict: ADOPT AS-IS — derive EVERY shared resource from the worktree
paths: app/scripts/run-tests.sh, ui/tests/lib/harness.js
terms: worktree-derived, derive the port, port collision, fixed port, shared cache, scratch root, dev state directory, per-worktree, simultaneous runs collide

area: phone-connection-runtime
section: 2.6
verdict: COPY THE APPROACH — the connection runtime and the outbox were named on 2026-09-18
paths: web-app/lib/link.js, src/phone/listen.rs, src/phone/mod.rs, phone/outbox
weak-paths: ui/phone.js, web-app/lib/
terms: outbox, reconnect, delivery retry, retry backoff, MAX_RETRY_MS, session reaper, could not be sent, intake

area: agent-contract
section: 2.7
verdict: the single most transplantable artifact in the repository — and it is prose, not code
paths: AGENTS.md
terms: agent contract

area: lint-layer-and-debt-ratchet
section: 3.2
verdict: full gap — we have NOTHING at the compiler/linter layer
terms: lint rule, lint plugin, custom lint, debt ratchet, per-file ceiling, oxlint
